  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 122 AM EDT Mon Aug 3 2026\n\nThe upper trough currently over our area continues to shift slowly \neast. However, models do not show a clean exit of the trough, but \nrather a weak upper low gets cuts off from the flow and left behind \nand then meanders over the region into mid-week.  Exactly where this \nlow is located matters for the details, but right now it appears we \nwill see at least scattered showers and thunderstorms around each \nday Monday through Wednesday, especially during the warmer afternoon \nhours when greatest instability occurs. With the ground already wet \nfrom recent rains across much of the area, broad marginal risks \nremain in place for isolated flooding potential each of the next \nseveral days.\n\nHow quickly the weak upper low departs is still unclear. However, \nmodels generally show us coming under increasing influence of \nlongwave troughing to our northwest, which would continue daily \nprecip chances even as the low becomes absorbed into mean flow \nsometime later in the week.\n\nTemperatures will be a bit below normal to start the work week, but \na gradual warming trend will bring temperatures back to around \nnormal by the Thursday through Sunday time frame.",
